problems.problem:
Find all functions $f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N}$ that satisfy the following three conditions:
a) $f(1)=1$;
b) $f(n+2)+\left(n^2+4n+3\right)f(n)=(2n+5)f(n+1)$ for all $n\in\mathbb{N}$;
c) $f(n)\mid f(m)$ for all natural $m>n$.

Let $H$ be the orthocenter of the acute-angled triangle $ABC$, and let the points $A_1$, $B_1$ and $C_1$ be the midpoints of $BC$, $CA$, $AB$ respectively. Let $A_2$ and $C_2$ be points such that $A_2A\perp AC$ and $A_2C_1\perp AB$, $C_2C\perp AC$ and $C_2A_1\perp BC$. Prove the following statements:
a) the midpoint of $BH$ lies on $A_2C_2$;
b) if $BB_1$ intersects the circumcircle of $A_1B_1C_1$ at the points $B_1$ and $B_3$, then $B_3$ lies on $A_2C_2$.

Let $n\geqslant1$ be an integer. A sequence $a_1,a_2,\ldots,a_{2n}$ is called lucky if
1) $a_1,\ldots,a_n$ is a permutation of $\{1,2,\ldots,n\}$;
2) $a_k=a_{n+k}$ for all $k=1,2,\ldots,n$;
3) there exist indices $1\leqslant i_1<i_2<\cdots<i_n\leqslant 2n$ such that $a_{i_k}=k$ for all $k=1,2,\ldots,n$.
For each $n\geqslant1$ find the number of lucky sequences.

Two players, Andrii and Olesia, play the following game. On the table there is a round cake, which one of them cuts into $4n$ sectors (pieces) of pairwise different weights. The weight of every piece is known to both players. After that they choose pieces for themselves according to the following rules. First Andrii takes $1$ piece, then Olesia takes 2 pieces, but in such a way that the pieces remaining on the table after her move form a sector. Then they take turns, taking 2 pieces each, so that after every move the pieces of the cake remaining on the table form a sector. With the last move Andrii takes the last piece. Each player wants the total weight of the part of the cake he or she has taken to be greater than that of the opponent. Can one of them guarantee to take more than half of the whole cake if:

a) the cake is cut into sectors by Olesia;

b) the cake is cut into sectors by Andrii, but with his first move he is not allowed to take the heaviest piece?
